    <section class="section" id="efficiency">
      <span class="section-label">Best Practices</span>
      <h2>Maximizing Efficiency</h2>
      <p>VibeCrew is designed to make each session more efficient than the last. Here's how to get the most out of the system and <em>why</em> each practice matters.</p>

      <h3>Context Window Discipline</h3>
      <p>VibeCrew targets <strong>&lt;50% context usage</strong> per session. Three mechanisms enforce this automatically:</p>
      <div class="card-grid">
        <div class="card">
          <div class="card-icon green">60%</div>
          <h4>Warning at 60%</h4>
          <p><code>check-context.sh</code> emits a warning. Start thinking about wrapping up. Commit working code.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <div class="card-icon amber">80%</div>
          <h4>Critical at 80%</h4>
          <p>Stronger warning. Commit and run <code>/wrap</code>. Consider delegating remaining work to a sub-agent.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <div class="card-icon red">90%</div>
          <h4>Danger at 90%</h4>
          <p>Agent quality severely degraded. Immediately run <code>/wrap</code>. Do not attempt complex operations.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p>When context compaction occurs, <code>compact-reinject.sh</code> automatically re-injects the current project state, active feature, and phase &mdash; so you don&rsquo;t lose context on what you were working on.</p>

      <h3>Token Efficiency</h3>
      <div class="card-grid">
        <div class="card">
          <h4>Use Context7 for everything</h4>
          <p>This is consistently the single biggest token saver. Every time you paste documentation into chat, you burn ~1,500 tokens that Context7 could fetch on-demand. The Performance Coach will flag this &mdash; listen to it.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h4>Write clear initial prompts</h4>
          <p>Prompt churn (-5 points each) comes from vague instructions. Spend 30 seconds writing a precise prompt instead of 3 follow-up corrections. The first prompt is the cheapest prompt.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h4>Respect context warnings</h4>
          <p>At 60%: start thinking about wrapping. At 80%: definitely wrap. Pushing to 100% forces a /clear and loses all accumulated understanding. A fresh context + CLAUDE.md = fast restart.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h4>Let agents run in isolation</h4>
          <p>Stack Scout, Builder, and Code Simplifier run in separate worktree context windows. Don&rsquo;t duplicate their work in the main session. Their research tokens never touch your context.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <h3>Workflow Efficiency</h3>
      <div class="card-grid">
        <div class="card">
          <h4>Always /wrap</h4>
          <p>Never close the terminal without wrapping. The session log feeds the learning system, the commit preserves your work, and the Vibe Score identifies improvements. Skipping /wrap means the session is invisible.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h4>Use /idea aggressively</h4>
          <p>Mid-feature inspiration should be captured, not pursued. /idea takes 1 second and produces 1 line of output. The idea is safe in the backlog. Your flow is preserved.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h4>Trust the phase gate</h4>
          <p>It feels restrictive at first. But the 15&ndash;30 minutes spent on the foundation prevents hours of rework from wrong architectural assumptions. Every seasoned VibeCrew user says the same thing: "I'm glad it forced me to plan."</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h4>Review CLAUDE.md monthly</h4>
          <p>Remove stale rules, consolidate duplicates, promote critical rules to the top. The Performance Coach adds rules &mdash; you curate them. A well-maintained CLAUDE.md is the most powerful file in your project.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <h3>A Typical Day</h3>
      <pre><code># Morning — start fresh
claude
# Session startup agent runs, shows where you left off

/status
# Dashboard: 2/4 features done, Trip Creation in progress

# Continue where you left off
"Continue implementing the itinerary builder component"

# Mid-session inspiration
/idea "add drag-and-drop reordering to itinerary items"

# Quick quality check
/check

# End of day
/wrap
# Vibe Score: 91/100. Coaching + commit + session log.</code></pre>

      <h3>Common Anti-Patterns to Avoid</h3>
      <TableWrap>
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr><th>Anti-Pattern</th><th>Consequence</th><th>Fix</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Skipping /wrap</strong></td>
              <td>No session log, no Vibe Score, no CLAUDE.md improvements, uncommitted work</td>
              <td>Always wrap. Make it a habit.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Ignoring the phase gate</strong></td>
              <td>AI makes wrong assumptions about stack, design, patterns</td>
              <td>Complete /new-project fully before any feature work</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Pasting docs into chat</strong></td>
              <td>1,500+ tokens wasted per paste, context fills faster</td>
              <td>Use Context7 MCP &mdash; it's configured and ready</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Starting features without specs</strong></td>
              <td>Feature Developer guesses at requirements, rework later</td>
              <td>Always run /plan-features or write a spec at /new-feature</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Pushing past 80% context</strong></td>
              <td>Context exhaustion, /clear, lose all session state</td>
              <td>Wrap at 80%. Fresh context + CLAUDE.md = fast restart</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Hardcoding design values</strong></td>
              <td>Inconsistent UI, wasted tokens fixing later</td>
              <td>Always reference design-system.css tokens</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </TableWrap>

      <h3>For Teams</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>One person owns the foundation.</strong> The project lead runs <code>/new-project</code> and establishes the TDR. Don't debate architecture in separate sessions.</li>
        <li><strong>Share the backlog.</strong> Use <code>/plan-features</code> as a team exercise. Commit <code>backlog.json</code> so everyone works from the same list.</li>
        <li><strong>Commit Vibe Scores.</strong> Make scores visible to the team &mdash; not as competition, but as a shared learning signal for retrospectives.</li>
        <li><strong>Standardize on Warp.</strong> The deep-link notifications are worth it when running parallel sessions across a team.</li>
        <li><strong>Do score retros.</strong> Even 15 minutes every two weeks reviewing score trends and adding team rules to CLAUDE.md compounds into massive efficiency gains.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

